{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{3}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)={1}^{3}+1=2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)={2}^{3}+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =150-6 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c29561ae78121ec9387e81bf827099b55816a4edc5b6baf3c7c1e3611e23af28"} {"question" "q-163599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "112\\,J"] ["math" "128\\,J"] ["math" "132\\,J"] ["math" "144\\,J"] ["math" "156\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "144\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(4t+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=4t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=2(0)^{2}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+t+3.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)^{2}+1+3=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)^{2}+2+3=13\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {13}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =338-72 \\\\  & =266\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c29561ae78121ec9387e81bf827099b55816a4edc5b6baf3c7c1e3611e23af28"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "226\\,J"] ["math" "236\\,J"] ["math" "246\\,J"] ["math" "256\\,J"] ["math" "266\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "266\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $5t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c29561ae78121ec9387e81bf827099b55816a4edc5b6baf3c7c1e3611e23af28"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "150\\,J"] ["math" "175\\,J"] ["math" "200\\,J"] ["math" "225\\,J"] ["math" "245\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "225\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6\\sqrt{x}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}6\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =4(8-1) \\\\  & =28\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $8\\,m/s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c29561ae78121ec9387e81bf827099b55816a4edc5b6baf3c7c1e3611e23af28"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,m/s"] ["math" "2\\,m/s"] ["math" "4\\,m/s"] ["math" "6\\,m/s"] ["math" "8\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\,m/s"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $3x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-4=1\\,m/s.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c29561ae78121ec9387e81bf827099b55816a4edc5b6baf3c7c1e3611e23af28"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,m/s"] ["math" "2\\,m/s"] ["math" "3\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"} {"question" "q-163701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060920/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac197df0f39c33153fd79a70d693552a6fe53731e9521b6432a530040d526260"}] "usage" {"q-163599" []} "basis" 1950 "retractions" [[17592186180033 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3t^{2} + 1)m/s^{2}$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186178199 17592186178200 17592186178201 17592186178202 17592186178203] "immutable_fields" [[17592186178198 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178198 :answer-field/id] [17592186178198 :answer-field/key] [17592186178198 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
